What these opening files are good for today

The files in openings and per-mod sets like save_the_queen.csv are excellent for repeatable regression checks.

A fixed suite is useful to detect “did this patch break known lines?”

This is especially important for your strict gating flow.

What they are not good enough for

A fixed opening list does not teach the engine by itself.

It mostly measures behavior on known trajectories.

If the same lines are reused repeatedly, tuning can overfit to those lines and still be weak elsewhere.

Recent behavior patterns you’ve been seeing (fixing one known game, then a new miss appears in another line) are classic signs of narrow-suite overfitting pressure.

“Make engine smarter” vs “make tests stricter”

Extending openings absolutely makes tests better and reduces blind spots.

It does not automatically make the engine smarter unless you also improve evaluation/search logic and validate against broader distributions.

So extending openings is necessary for smarter development loops, but not sufficient on its own.

Why extension is important now

You are running high-discipline gate flows where a patch can pass targeted probes but fail 50-game batch on new lines.

That usually means line diversity is still too low relative to the behavior space.

Mod-specific rule systems increase branch complexity, so each mod needs broader “rule-pressure” opening diversity than classical chess alone.

What “enough” should look like in practice

Keep a fixed deterministic gate slice per mod for comparability.

Add a larger rotating discovery slice per mod to prevent memorization effects.

Ensure structural diversity:
opening families, early central breaks, flank structures, tactical forcing lines, quiet development lines, asymmetrical pawn structures, king-exposure trajectories.

Ensure role diversity:
both sides to move in critical early branches, not only one side’s comfort positions.

Ensure mod-rule diversity:
each mod’s unique rule pressure should appear early and often, not only in a few recurring lines.

Ensure de-duplication quality:
different UCI prefixes that transpose into near-identical positions should not dominate the suite.

A practical target profile

Fixed gate slice: stable small set for strict regression.

Rotating discovery slice: larger set changed periodically to prevent overfit.

Stress slice: deliberately adversarial lines designed from prior failures.

This three-layer structure gives you both stability and exploration.

Verdict on your question

Yes, extending the opening suites is the right move.

Do it to increase coverage and reduce overfitting risk in your improvement loop.

Treat it as test-distribution hardening, not as a direct strength upgrade mechanism.